{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LZhfHJcdzkpxkLBuFrph3E","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, the directory exists. Now let me write the complete LaTeX paper. I need to carefully incorporate all 10 experiments from experiment_data.json:\n\n1. ner_n16_scierc - NER N=16 on SciERC, N-scaling\n2. ner_conll2003 - NER on CoNLL-2003\n3. ner_ontonotes - NER on OntoNotes 5.0\n4. re_diagnostic_analysis - RE per-relation-type breakdown\n\n\n5. temperature_sensitivity_analysis - T=0.7 vs T=1.0\n6. adaptive_compute_demo - SJ-based best-of-N selection\n7. ner_multi_model - LLaMA-3.1-8B cross-model\n8. re_n16_confirmation - RE N=16 confirmation\n9. capability_stratification - capability-stratified rho analysis\n10. selection_curves - NER best-of-N selection curves\n\nI see there are actually 11 experiments total including the OOD robustness one. Now I'm outlining the paper structure with sections for Abstract, Introduction, Related Work, and the Method section covering the Structural Self-Consistency framework.\n\nFor the experiments section, I'm organizing the results by theme: starting with the core NER performance across datasets, then the N-scaling effects, cross-model validation with different architectures, out-of-distribution robustness, temperature sensitivity analysis, relation extraction diagnostics, capability stratification analysis, and finally the adaptive compute selection strategies. I'm mapping each result to its corresponding table and now working through how to organize the tables themselves, starting with notation and setup in Table 1, then N-scaling comparisons on SciERC...\n\nWriting the LaTeX source... formatting so it compiles with pdflatex without any extra .sty files.\n\nLet me carefully construct this paper with all the numbers from the data.\n\nKey numbers to use:\n\n**NER SciERC (N=16)**:\n- greedy F1: 0.667, oracle F1: 0.812, headroom: 0.145\n- rho_sj: 0.448, rho_fk: 0.298, delta: 0.15\n- p_sj: 2e-27, n=529\n\n**NER SciERC (N=8, from the analysis, rho was 0.383)**:\n- N=8 rho_sj: 0.383 (inferred from analysis: \"rho 从 0.383→0.448\")\n- N=8 oracle_headroom: 0.108 from the temperature analysis\n- n_scaling_rho_gain: 0.065\n\nNow I'm pulling together the metrics for the other NER datasets and the relation extraction diagnostic. For CoNLL-2003 with N=8, I have greedy F1 at 0.893 and oracle F1 at 0.928 with much tighter headroom of 0.035, plus strong correlations with rho_sj at 0.412 across 3453 examples. OntoNotes shows similar patterns with greedy F1 of 0.782 and oracle at 0.856, headroom of 0.074, and rho_sj of 0.395 across a larger set of 8262 examples. For the relation extraction diagnostic, I'm working with 7 relation types.\n\nNow looking at the temperature analysis results across different settings. The NER performance on SciERC shows rho values around 0.383-0.388 at T=0.7 with headroom around 0.055, and at T=1.0 the rho drops slightly to 0.383 but headroom increases to 0.108. For relation extraction at T=0.7, the full rho is 0.218 but conditional rho jumps to 0.372, and at T=1.0 the full rho increases to 0.244 while conditional rho drops to 0.316.\n\nMoving into the adaptive compute experiments with 16 samples on NER, I'm seeing that the SJ strategy achieves 0.706 F1, which is a 3.9 percentage point gain over greedy and captures about 27% of the available headroom. The oracle reaches 0.812, and interestingly the random baseline at 0.682 performs better than FK at 0.693. For the multi-model setup with LLaMA on NER, SJ's rho is 0.368 compared to FK's 0.285, giving an 0.083 delta. On the relation extraction side with 16 samples, greedy achieves 0.421 F1 with an oracle of 0.648 and headroom of 0.227, where SJ's full rho reaches 0.268.\n\nNow I'm looking at how performance varies across different capability strata. For NER, I'm breaking down the results by F1 ranges—entities with zero F1 have 24 samples, those between 0 and 0.3 have 42 samples with rho of 0.28, the 0.3-0.6 range has 85 samples with rho of 0.41, the 0.6-0.9 range shows 178 samples with rho of 0.52, and high-performing entities above 0.9 F1 have 200 samples with rho of 0.38. For relation extraction, the zero F1 stratum contains 112 samples, the 0-0.3 range has 68 samples with rho of 0.18, and the 0.3-0.6 range has 72 samples.\n\nLooking at the selection curves for NER on SciERC, I'm comparing how different strategies scale as I increase the number of samples. SJ and FK both show steady improvement from 0.665 at 1 sample up to 0.706 and 0.693 respectively at 16 samples, while random selection plateaus more slowly. The oracle curve demonstrates the ceiling—reaching 0.812 at 16 samples—showing there's substantial room for improvement with better selection strategies.\n\nNow examining the out-of-distribution robustness results. The in-distribution correlation is 0.395 for SJ, but it drops to 0.342 out-of-distribution, representing a 13.4% degradation. Even with oracle selection, the OOD F1 only reaches 0.782 compared to the greedy baseline of 0.685, leaving a 0.097 headroom. SJ outperforms FK by 0.068, confirming it's the stronger approach. Ready to write up these findings.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":209,"cache_read_input_tokens":36790,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":209},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9e647442-6c53-4f93-a52e-879487483642","uuid":"46e30cca-23eb-440b-9de5-6ce0faeebb4c"}
